WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.250
Hello, Conan O'Brien here.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.110
For the past two weeks,
we've been featuring

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.300
prominent black voices on our program,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.180
and I would like to continue that tonight.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.310
Earlier today, I had a
fantastic conversation

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.170
with Senator Cory Booker.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.290
I do not wanna waste any time,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.140
so let's get right to that interview.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.510
My guest tonight was the
mayor of Newark, New Jersey

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.810
from 2006 to 2013.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:30.630
He's also the first African
American senator from New Jersey

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.460
and was a candidate for
the Democratic nomination

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.600
in the 2020 presidential election.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.010
I am very honored to
welcome Senator Cory Booker,

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.210
Senator, how are you?

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.070
I'm well, Conan, thank you
for having me on again.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.460
I want you to know I put
on a nice shirt for you,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.970
this is my talking to a senator shirt.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.900
God, we could've done
an agreement beforehand,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.260
a mutual disarmament.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.400
And I would (audio cuts
out) shirt on and relaxed.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.700
When I wear a tie, I look
like a kid these days

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.150
that found his dad's tie.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.100
So I'm trying to show you
respect, but not overdo it.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.230
Well, maybe one day, you'll
show me the ultimate respect,

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.880
and for an interview in the
future, you will be bald.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:13.972
'Cause that would--

00:01:13.972 --> 00:01:14.943
(Conan laughs)

00:01:14.943 --> 00:01:18.560
That would be the greatest
act of respect and deference.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.160
I'll tell you, Senator,
it's a good look for you,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.620
it would not be a good
look for me, all right.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.600
You can pull it off.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.010
Thank you for doing this,
I know you must be slammed

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.900
and really busy, but it's
a thrill to talk to you.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.020
Because there's so much
everybody is trying

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.310
to process and understand at the moment.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.310
One of the things I wanted to
start with is that we both,

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:49.650
you and I, lived through the
riots following the release

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.800
of the video of Rodney
King's beating and the riots

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.800
that followed the police
officers being let go.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.460
And I was here in Los Angeles at the time.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.440
You lived through those
riots, I know, as well.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.240
And am I correct, these,
what's happened since

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.910
the death of George Floyd
feels very different.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.760
Does it feel different to you?

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.440
Well, in some ways, I know
that moment very well,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.710
it was probably one of the
most explosive personal moments

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:28.710
in my life where I often
channeled energy and anger

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.170
in every way I could to
try to advance issues,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.480
I was a college student, but
I had lost control that night,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.150
I wrote a column for
Stanford's newspaper about,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.610
Why Have I Lost Control
was the title of it.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:43.610
And it was just this
explosion of a lifetime worth

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.240
of experiences where you're
followed and surveilled

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.660
and stopped and had guns drawn on you,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.920
and here I was at a point where
I was president of my class,

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.900
football player at Stanford,
on my way to Oxford to study,

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.980
and I just felt like
I'm still at this point

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.580
where black men in America
is putting their lives

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.420
at risk every time they
have an interaction.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:08.390
And here, when I saw clearly
on a video tape 50 plus blows

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.870
to this man that everybody of
good conscience can just say

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.880
that was wrong that you had that verdict.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.740
And here I was again, 30 years
later, I remember that night,

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.763
I remember marching in the
streets in northern California.

00:03:22.504 --> 00:03:26.160
And this feeling came up in
me again after Ahmaud Arbery,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.080
jogging in his community, Breonna Taylor,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.250
sleeping in her home, a guy birdwatching

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.500
was threatened with
something that black men know

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.060
could result in beating, being beaten,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.103
being arrested, being thrown in jails.

00:03:38.996 --> 00:03:40.530
And then you see this torture of

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.520
a human being until they die.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.180
And it's just been a time
where you're broken hearted,

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.900
not just if you're black.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.530
I just am a big believer, if America

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.430
hasn't broken your heart,
you don't love her enough,

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.860
when this kind of stuff
goes on and has now,

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.130
that's been about 30 years ago.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:58.900
So am I hopeful?

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.070
I think you have to be a prisoner of hope.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.320
Because America in an of
itself is a testimony to people

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.870
under the most wretched
conditions hoping for better.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:13.650
And so yeah, it's amazing
for me to see 50 states plus

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.883
a dozen countries, people
protesting, small towns,

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.080
big cities, diversity of age, of race,

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.300
and then be in a position like a senator

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.160
where I could write a bill that could not,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.930
I'm telling you right now,
could not have been written

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.430
a month ago and get the
majority of the Democrats

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.460
in the House and the
Senate to sign on quickly.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.430
Couldn't have been done if
it wasn't for the protests.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.030
So yeah, I'm really hopeful,
because there's one thing

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.260
I know, and I know you love history,

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.150
there's one truism that's true
about America's advancement.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.200
From the suffrage
movement to workers' right

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.430
to civil rights to LGBTQ rights,
is that change doesn't come

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.670
from Washington, it comes to Washington.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.670
All of those great
advancements were preceded

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.940
by Americans getting up off of the couch

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.470
and getting involved
in protest, in pushing,

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.490
in demanding that their
government leap forward,

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.930
and this has got to be
one of those moments.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:09.930
I often, people ask me about
history or the country,

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.420
I always say the same thing,
America's a work in progress.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.760
It's always been a work in progress.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.800
And there's a lot to do,
and then unfortunately,

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.430
it almost feels that we've
experienced way too many

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.610
of these moments where we
realize how much we have to do,

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.290
how much work we have to do.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.330
Well, I think it's a disconnection

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.880
where we have a poverty
of empathy for each other.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.330
And King in his letter
from Birmingham Jail,

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.680
I posted on my Instagram
account this one section

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.250
where he says, what worries
me more than the Ku Klux Clan

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:43.920
and the White Citizens' Council

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.200
is actually white
moderates, he was saying.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.450
Call this out, this idea that people

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:53.358
who don't feel moved
enough to get involved

00:05:53.358 --> 00:05:56.330
in changing the injustices that
they would themselves admit.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.480
And so we're at this moment,
I think, where it is a test

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.750
to will we address that
poverty of empathy.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.370
And I see signs that we
are, because these problems

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:08.760
cannot be solved with the same
levels of empathy and love

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.650
with which, that allowed
them to fester for so long.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.600
We need to raise the level of love,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.700
and love is not an anemic
word, it's not saccharine,

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.560
love is actually sacrifice and service

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.310
for those who are suffering or struggling.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:27.000
And so this to me is
really one of those moments

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.800
where we are at a crossroads
in American history.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.070
And my hope is that this
becomes one of those big moments

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.800
where we all look back and,
when we're asked by our children

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.560
or grandchildren, what did
you do when America was

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:40.816
at a crossroads?

00:06:40.816 --> 00:06:45.816
Did you sit at home and
hope and wish and cheer

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.050
on the sidelines, or did
you get on the streets

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.220
or get engaged in a way
that made it happen?

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:55.360
And that's my prayer, because
my grandmother, black woman,

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:56.830
I would ask her those questions,

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:57.980
where were you when this happened?

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.260
I still remember, World War II,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.260
she wasn't storming beaches in Normandy,

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.110
but she bragged to me that
she had victory gardens

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.530
and she bought war bonds and
she was a part of the effort.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.850
That's when we're at our
best, when we're all invested,

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.340
and that's still the test
that's going on right now,

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.840
is will we all be invested?

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.630
I know, I've been trying to talk

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.793
to people about their personal stories.

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:23.540
I know that you have the
experience of your parents talking

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:27.550
to you when you were 12 or 13 years old

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:31.890
about being careful when
you went out in the world

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:35.273
because the color of your
skin puts you in peril.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.080
Yeah, I think that
that's the thing I've...

00:07:42.037 --> 00:07:43.930
And I hate to use this word, but I feel

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.390
a lot of shame about.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:49.300
Because my parents did the right
thing for a young boy that,

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.850
by the time he was 12 or
13 was over six feet tall,

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:56.280
and that they knew that this
world would potentially see me

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:57.310
as a threat.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.120
And I had conversations early on,

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.650
especially around the time
I got a driver's license,

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:06.010
where my parents not just
told me to be careful,

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:10.650
but made me sufficiently
afraid to protect my life.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.950
And I guess when I say
shame, I'm not shameful

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:15.580
about my parents were of
a generation where they,

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:19.790
their circles had stories
about vicious interactions

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:22.530
with police and authorities.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.740
But I think the shame that I'm talking to

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:27.130
is that I've had 30 years of adulthood now

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:33.130
and now I have to tell my
nephews and my mentees,

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:34.460
I have to give them the same lesson

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.670
that we shouldn't have to
give in America to kids.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:43.670
And so it hurts a lot that this tradition,

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.640
this awful, wretched tradition,

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:48.410
continues in this country,
and it needs to end.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.740
I was speaking to a friend of mine,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.160
the comedian Ron Funches
on last night's show.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.420
His son's autistic,
and he said, for years,

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.480
he's worried that his son
would act out in public.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.160
And because of the color of his skin,

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.433
his life would be at risk if
the police had to intervene.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.050
But it's not an irrational fear.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:09.630
People are shot holding a cell phone,

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.523
people are shot reaching for a pen.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:16.920
And then those officers
who use that awful,

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.963
unleash that awful violence,
can say that they were afraid.

00:09:22.052 --> 00:09:24.850
So I, this has been a
week where I've talked

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.460
to a lot of my African American
friends who are parents,

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:32.223
and every conversation
just hurts at a level that,

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.070
it's just difficult and you feel weary

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:39.223
and you feel exhausted and sick and tired.

00:09:40.506 --> 00:09:42.930
But at the same time, you
know you have to engage

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:47.930
in an impossible alchemy that
better generations than us

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:51.900
have engaged in, that somehow
you can transform that hurt

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:56.150
and that pain and even
trauma for many into change,

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:00.900
into an expansion of the
consciousness of others, into love.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.600
And so many communities
in America have had to,

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.600
been forced to bring
forward that rare magic

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.577
where you have to turn hate and complicity

00:10:15.491 --> 00:10:18.460
into action to save your own lives

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.310
and to save the soul of your country.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:25.980
I wanna specifically
talk about the police,

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:30.980
because this is a conversation
in the last two weeks

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:34.632
that's morphing and changing all the time.

00:10:34.632 --> 00:10:37.100
And it feels now we're in a moment

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:42.100
of talking about how
specifically can we do something

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.040
to make sure that these deadly conflicts

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:50.810
with the police are, don't exist,

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.290
which might be a pie in the sky dream,

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:55.650
but are certainly much less frequent.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:59.880
And you have a bill that you've put forth,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.290
the Justice in Policing Act of 2020.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:07.100
What are the specific things in that bill

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:09.090
that you think could make a difference?

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:10.530
So I wanna confess to you, it may be

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.200
the broadest police
accountability bill ever

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:14.600
in the history of our country.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:17.730
But it's very narrow,
so it's stopping stuff

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.130
that's just common sense
you shouldn't, choke holds,

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:23.360
racial, religious profiling,
no knock warrants,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:24.860
a whole bunch of these practices,

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:27.130
in some cities, they're banned,

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.420
that should be the law
of the land for America.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:33.040
Number two, it just says,
hey, if you really violate

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.070
the law, if you engage
in criminal behavior,

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:38.350
you should be held
accountable for that behavior.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:41.350
And so it creates a better platform

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:43.890
for criminal prosecution
on the federal level,

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.830
a standard that's achievable for people

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:48.170
who do seriously wrong, and if you violate

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:51.220
someone's civil rights, that you no longer

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:52.630
have something called qualified immunity.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.010
And then the last thing,
which is just so obvious

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:58.180
to me as well, is that sunshine

00:11:58.180 --> 00:11:59.450
is the ultimate disinfectant.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:03.210
If every department has to
report up their uses of force,

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:06.810
their police misconduct,
it creates the ability

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.440
for an average citizen all the way

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:10.250
to a person in the Senate to be able

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.330
to hold a department
accountable and stop some things

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:15.480
that are bizarre in this country,

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:17.830
where someone in one city can create,

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:20.420
do awful accidents of police misconduct,

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.080
quit that department, move
to another city and get hired

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.790
without that new department
even knowing about their record.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:30.080
So this is a broad, strong
bill, but I'm gonna tell you,

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:32.350
when I say it's narrowly tailored,

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:36.730
it does not ultimately
solve the larger problems

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:39.710
of our society, what I
mean by that is, King said,

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.960
I can't legislate you to love me,

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:43.590
but I can stop you from lynching me.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.360
And so this is a bill that serves to stop

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.610
what we saw with Eric
Garner and Breonna Taylor,

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.080
I can go through that, but
what is the larger call

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:54.240
of activists, this is the
beauty in a lot of things

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.860
that I'm hearing right now.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.550
Is again, back to that
issue, is what is the quality

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.680
of our mercy, what is the
level of our love in America?

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:03.280
Because everybody, I can talk to people

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:04.510
on both sides of the isle.

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:06.760
I've talked to law enforcement officers.

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.310
My first meeting with the FBI
when I was mayor of my city

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:11.700
when we were going through
gang intelligence and I asked

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.810
this guy, just to see what
his response would be,

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:15.140
how do we solve this problem?

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:16.920
And the head of the FBI says to me,

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:19.320
we don't solve this problem.

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:21.870
Because the law enforcement
officials in our country

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.770
are dealing with symptoms, they're dealing

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:26.360
with our lack of love and
mercy towards each other.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:28.710
20% of our incarcerations are for people

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:32.150
with drug addictions and suffering
with drug abuse problems,

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.100
and we don't give them
help, we give them prison

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.030
and jails, put them back on the streets,

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.010
they get rearrested multiple times,

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:39.830
costing us hundreds of
thousands of dollars

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:41.840
before they might find
their way into treatment.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.100
People with mental illness.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:47.220
We criminalize them for a healthcare issue

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.620
and don't get them the treatment,

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:50.530
we throw them in our prisons and jails.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:53.010
Low income people, we
have criminalized poverty

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:53.990
in this country.

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.700
Any American knows when you have

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:57.310
to make this terrible decision, do I pay

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:00.460
for a traffic violation,
which now has resulted

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:04.270
in a warrant for my
arrest, or do I pay my rent

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:06.410
or put food on the table for my kids?

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:09.620
We have been defunding public education,

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:11.420
we have been defunding the kind of things,

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:14.730
I can show you the data
that, social scientists

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:17.300
can show you, prove that
it would lower crime

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:19.240
and our need for police
in the first place.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.760
So my bill that I wrote with Kamala Harris

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.910
and the head of the Congressional
Black Caucus and Nadler,

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.920
an incredible coalition to
just hold police accountable

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.690
is important, but what we
should be striving for,

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:34.170
don't we have a greater moral
imagination in our country

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.410
that we can, that we've
created a police state.

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:40.160
We are 4% to 5% of the globe's population,

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.820
but one out of every
four incarcerated people

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.000
on the planet Earth is
in the land of the free.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.140
One out of every three incarcerated women

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.400
on the planet Earth is
in the land of the free,

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:51.440
and it's such a racially biased system

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.300
that we are now at a point in America

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:55.250
where there are more African Americans

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:58.050
under criminal supervision, prison, jail,

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:01.230
probation, parole, than
all the slaves in 1850.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:02.770
What is the quality of our mercy,

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:04.310
what is the level of our love?

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:06.420
That's what the artists
of activism on the streets

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:08.260
are beginning to call for.

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:11.740
Can't we be a society that stops
problem before they happen,

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:13.367
and then we throw it all on to our police

00:15:13.367 --> 00:15:17.000
and our prisons, never really
truly affirming human dignity

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.970
and actualizing human potential?

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:21.960
It feels to me, I had a
conversation the other day

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:26.360
with W. Kamau Bell, who made a point

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.430
that has resonated with me ever since,

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:29.860
that's what I go to sleep thinking about

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:31.930
and what I wake up thinking about,

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:35.880
which is this is about all of
us doing our work, meaning,

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.750
rather than, yes, there need
to be institutional changes

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:43.160
in some of the ways
that we enforce the law,

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:44.730
there needs to be accountability,

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:47.010
these are things that are clearly true.

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:52.010
But it is false to say this
is all up to other people,

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:56.990
meaning for me to sit back and
say, someone fix the police

00:15:56.990 --> 00:16:00.290
and then I'm here, I'm
just gonna watch the news

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:01.560
and see if that happens.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:04.040
Everybody has to be accountable,

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:08.540
everybody has to practice empathy
every day and sensitivity.

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:13.540
It's a practice the way yoga's a practice

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:18.010
or any other exercise is
a practice or religion

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:19.990
is a practice, it's
something that you have

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:22.050
to engage in all the time.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:25.040
Yeah, Conan, I'm here in
part because of a white guy

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:27.670
who was doing nothing on a
couch in New Jersey four years

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:30.080
before I was born, 65, he was watching

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:31.730
a movie back when we had three channels,

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:35.060
most of America's watching
Judgment at Nuremberg.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:38.060
The movie is cut into
because of something going on

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:40.310
on a bridge called the
Edmund Pettus Bridge.

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:43.060
He watches John Lewis and
others being beat viciously,

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:46.140
we know it as Bloody
Sunday, and a white guy

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:49.730
in the suburbs on a couch
stands up, he's so moved,

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:52.700
he doesn't allow his
inability to do everything

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:54.620
to undermine his
determination to do something,

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:56.900
and he decides to go
to Alabama, then alas,

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.380
he can't afford a plane ticket,
he just started a business.

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:01.390
But he says, you know
what, one hour a week,

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.160
I'm gonna do something,
and he calls and he finds

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:04.710
these activists that are helping

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:07.440
to break housing segregation,
because black families

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:09.720
are being turned away from
the suburbs in New Jersey.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.410
And he goes to, four years later,

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:12.810
he's got a sting operation set up,

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:14.830
he's representing a family.

00:17:14.830 --> 00:17:16.880
They were turned away from a house,

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:20.010
a white couple posing as
them gets to buy the house,

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:21.790
they put a bid on, on
the day of the closing,

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:24.920
lawyer, black man, attacked
by the real estate agent

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:27.170
with dogs, punched in the face.

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:28.470
Lo and behold, they eventually move

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.000
into this incredible nurturing home

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.950
and a great community
with great public schools.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:33.283
That was my family.

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:37.270
That guy represented my family,
because in a moral moment,

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:39.460
he stood up and said, I'm just
gonna give an hour a week.

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:41.750
If he didn't do that, I
might not be sitting here

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:42.860
having this conversation.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:45.670
And to say any of us are powerless

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:47.990
is to surrender our power.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:52.500
To say any of us are not
responsible is to be complicit

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:54.820
in the very injustice which we criticize.

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:57.550
And so the question is,
what are you gonna do?

00:17:57.550 --> 00:18:00.840
All of us, we have to, I've
got more texts from people

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:02.630
of good faith, in good will, saying,

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:03.980
I don't know what to do, what can I do?

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.710
And my response has not been
to tell them what to do.

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:09.360
My response is to never
let go of that question.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.920
Every day, wrestle with that
question, let it haunt you,

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.140
let it push you to always
say I'm doing something,

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.160
something in the cause of my country.

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:20.170
Because we all, every
one of us, drink deeply

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.560
from wells of freedom and liberty

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:24.510
and privilege that we didn't dig.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.850
We are here because of people
that were willing to die.

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:31.670
We are all, no matter who we are, this,

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.740
what we're living is our
ancestors' wildest dreams.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:36.980
But you cheapen it and degrade it

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:38.870
if you don't continue with that commitment

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:42.280
so that our great grandchildren
will one day be living

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.670
in an America beyond our
dreams that really does,

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.760
as a nation that's in search of herself,

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.610
really does make real on the promise

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:52.570
that we have liberty and justice for all.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:57.260
You've spoken a lot about empathy.

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:02.260
And it's impossible for me not to mention,

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:07.520
we've actually had almost two
weeks of shows I believe now

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:09.860
where we've not once
mentioned the president

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:12.210
of the United States,
because I haven't wanted him

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:14.290
to be a part of the conversation.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:17.290
I've wanted the conversation
to go deeper than that.

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:19.950
But I'll bring him up now,
because we have a leader

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:24.680
of the country who I
think everyone would agree

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:29.260
has a chronic inability
to demonstrate empathy.

00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:34.260
And I feel that that has been
a situation like what happened

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.010
after the killing of George Floyd is,

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.910
as crazy as it sounds, it's
an opportunity for change,

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:43.130
it's an opportunity for the
president of the United States

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:46.640
to bring us all together
and to say beautiful things

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:50.040
that might actually bring
about lasting change.

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:54.520
And I feel that that has been
squandered by our president.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.391
Yeah, so I love you for not centering him

00:19:57.391 --> 00:20:00.470
in your conversations,
because it's not about him,

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.920
this election is coming
up, it's not a referendum

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:04.960
on one guy and one office,
it's really a referendum

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:07.720
on who we are and who we're
gonna be to each other.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:10.900
And we've got to raise the
frequency in our nation

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:13.370
in terms of the love and
empathy, but you mentioned him,

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:14.360
so let me just say this.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.970
I just fundamentally believe,
and I've watched leaders

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:21.650
on both sides of the
isle demonstrate this.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:24.300
You can't lead the people if
you don't love the people.

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:27.910
And he just does not love all American,

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:30.370
you don't have to agree
with all Americans,

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:34.720
but his willingness to so cavalierly deny

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:39.320
so many Americans human
dignity, for him to trample upon

00:20:41.067 --> 00:20:43.760
the very humanity of other Americans,

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.520
to cheapen this nation by working so hard

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:48.960
to divide us and to
make us hate each other.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:51.460
All of these things
mean he is illegitimate

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:54.690
in the kind of moral
leadership that we need.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.050
He may have the trappings of power,

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:59.140
he may have the title of the office,

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.170
but the title doesn't make the man,

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:05.370
the man has to make the title,
and he has been a failure,

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:08.610
a failure in a moral
office that Republicans

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:13.610
from Lincoln to Democrats like Obama,

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:17.390
who have led with love for their country,

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:19.650
with malice towards none and
with charity towards all,

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.590
he's had so much malice towards so many

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:24.370
and so little charity towards others.

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:28.200
So I just want this to be
a moral moment in America

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:31.800
and I'm so proud of his
former cabinet members

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:35.070
and national Republicans
who are coming forward

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:36.830
and saying just that, that this man

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:39.740
is uniquely unqualified
for this moment in history.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:44.290
And he has cheapened, to
me, the greatest ideal

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:48.000
in all of humanity which
America has striven

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.480
in every generation to make
manifest in this world,

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:53.480
is that we are a nation of
love where we love one another,

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.630
he has so failed to be in the fight

00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:58.910
to make us a more loving
nation, or as King would say,

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:03.373
a more beloved community,
and I am, I have so much hurt

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:08.160
for what he's done to people.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.950
And I've watched it, I've watched people,

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:15.050
when he calls some countries
shithole countries.

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:17.150
I've seen people, immigrants, come to me,

00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:18.800
begging me to do something about it,

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:23.800
I've watched him degrade, I
talked to a college friend

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:29.597
of mine whose kid was in
Lafayette Park when he stormed in,

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:37.120
had people storm in with
rubber bullets and tear gas.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:38.680
And he anguished that
somebody would think,

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:42.210
in this sacred capital
where people, from the March

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.320
on Washington to Second
Amendment rights people

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.850
have come for generations
to peacefully petition

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.420
their government, and he
just tramples that for what,

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:51.120
a national security issue because he had

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:52.280
to urgently get someone?

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:55.030
Nope, to hold up a book
that he should read

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:58.270
that talks about humility and the meek.

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.810
So I can't give him more of my energy,

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.670
I wanna give it to us,
I wanna give it to love,

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:06.350
I wanna give it to good
people who right now

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:08.680
don't understand the
urgency for them to act.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:10.940
That's where I wanna give
it, and so I don't wanna,

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:13.530
he is not the center of my concerns.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.530
And I appreciate you having
a show that reflects that.

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:21.160
Well, I have not wanted to do Trump jokes

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:27.610
or make it all about Donald
Trump, because ultimately,

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:32.610
I find him very uninteresting
and his actions sadden me.

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.930
But I'm angry, I reserve my anger

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:40.560
for people that enable him.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:44.250
That's what makes me
angry, and I get very angry

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:48.950
at people who do know
better, who are in a party

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:52.950
that may be, in the short
term, benefiting from the fact

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:56.050
that he holds that office,
but in the long term,

00:23:56.050 --> 00:23:57.190
I don't know how they're gonna be able

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:59.120
to talk to their kids,
to their grandchildren.

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:02.270
And so I'd been very moved by,

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:05.040
especially the military
leadership that's come forward,

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:09.760
Mattis, Kelly, most recently Colin Powell.

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:12.200
That is something that
the Republican Party

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:17.200
does show respect for, is
our military leadership,

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:18.760
and when you have these prominent members

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:21.730
of the military speaking out, that,

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:23.640
my heart swells with pride.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:27.210
And I'm very happy that
people are coming forward,

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:30.270
I just wish there were more,
I wish there were more people

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:32.900
who would step forward and
say, you know what, yes,

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:35.780
we got some good court appointments, yes,

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:39.560
we got some stuff done
because we had someone

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:42.320
in the Oval Office who
would do our bidding,

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:44.682
but this is too much.

00:24:44.682 --> 00:24:48.960
I wish we'd hear from more
politicians like that.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:52.253
Well, we've heard throughout
history, the bystander.

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:56.150
King talked about, we have to
repent in our day and age not

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:57.710
for the, simply for the vitriolic words

00:24:57.710 --> 00:24:59.150
and violent actions of the bad people,

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:01.240
but the silence and
inaction of the good people.

00:25:01.240 --> 00:25:04.330
So I think that you're right
and I think that this, again,

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:06.610
is that moment in American history.

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:09.710
And I actually feel sorry for
the Republican Party because,

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:13.930
as a lot of his primary opponents
in 2016, 15 and 16, said,

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:15.720
they predicted, Lindsey
Graham, all of them,

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:17.800
predicted he would destroy
the Republican Party.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.380
And a lot of them have gone silent now

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:22.940
and are refusing to tell
the truth of the perversion,

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:26.030
the meanness, that he's
cavalier in the meanness

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:28.100
that isn't at the heart
of a lot of Republicans,

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:30.210
you named some of them, like Colin Powell.

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:34.810
And so this is ultimately a
test, and that test isn't over.

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:36.850
This is a president who could disrespect

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:40.300
the peaceful transition of
power in 150 days if he loses,

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:42.820
he's already trying to
illegitimize the election.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:44.430
This is a president that is capable,

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:46.400
has shown us that he's
capable of anything.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:48.720
And so the stress test on this nation

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:51.823
over the next 150, 160
days are gonna be great.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:56.230
And this is a defining
moment for all of us.

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:59.780
And in a defining moment,
you're not defined

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:01.090
by what happens to you.

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:02.720
This guy's capable of anything.

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:05.480
You are always defined by
how you choose to respond.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:09.150
And I agree with you, I'm happy for those

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:11.960
who have decided to
respond by not silence,

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:14.230
but by speaking up, by
lifting their voices

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:16.010
to be heard by history.

00:26:16.010 --> 00:26:21.010
I keep, and I hate to
be, I don't wanna have

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:24.810
a false note of optimism, but
I've been looking for that,

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:28.920
and I've, we started out this conversation

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.383
by talking about Rodney King.

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:36.550
That felt like it was a
moment that then passed.

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:39.450
This feels like it has the
potential to be a moment

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:42.430
that lasts, a moment that continues.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:45.530
I'd like it to be, I'd
like it to be a moment

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:48.180
where we don't say, okay,
we made it through that

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:50.080
and now things have calmed down.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:55.020
I'd like to believe that
there's gonna be lasting change.

00:26:55.020 --> 00:26:59.160
And I do think the fact that we are now,

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:03.040
it's been over two weeks
and we are still seeing

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:08.040
an incredible amount of
unity in the reaction to how,

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:13.070
to George Floyd's death,
but also just the history

00:27:13.070 --> 00:27:15.460
of how common this was.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:18.470
This was not a surprise to
black people, his death,

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:23.210
it was sadly, it shocked
many other people,

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:26.310
but sadly was not a shock to people

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:28.750
in the African American community.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:30.250
Not at all.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:31.980
And I think the African American community

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:36.980
has had to find a way to work
through disappointment over

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:43.880
and over again and betrayal
over and over again.

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:45.380
And I just think about this last week,

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:47.920
where Senator Harris
and I were on the floor

00:27:49.683 --> 00:27:52.903
to try to meet a moment
where all the House,

00:27:52.903 --> 00:27:55.790
members of the House of
Representatives but four voted

00:27:55.790 --> 00:28:00.470
for an anti-lynching bill
that 99 senators understand

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:02.500
that this has been a bill that

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:06.260
has been tried over 200 times,
well over a century to pass,

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:08.160
failing over and over
again, that this country

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:09.420
with the most shameful history

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:11.370
that we don't teach adequately

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:13.653
in high school or even college.

00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:17.120
But the greatest periods
of domestic terrorism

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:19.040
we've ever seen, thousands
of African Americans

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:22.880
being lynched, not to mention
disappearing or being beaten.

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:25.630
And we were on the verge
of passing yet again

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:29.069
and we failed, because a
senator objected to it.

00:28:29.069 --> 00:28:33.040
And so I just think that
that's the moment we have

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:35.870
to recognize, civil
rights legislation failed

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:38.393
for decades and decades until it passed.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:42.180
Suffrage movement failed over and over

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:43.990
and over again until it passed.

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:46.730
And the people that can
work through heartbreak

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:48.560
and recognize the heart,
that's that one organ

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:50.910
that keeps beating even
though it's broken,

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.240
that that's what we
need to be sustained by.

00:28:53.240 --> 00:28:55.210
So I'm a prisoner of hope just like you,

00:28:55.210 --> 00:28:59.750
but hope will get slain.

00:28:59.750 --> 00:29:01.400
In fact, the message,
where King was killed,

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:05.330
if you go to Lorraine Motel
right now and look down,

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:09.900
and it's words from the
Torah and from the Bible

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:12.440
are written there where King
was slain, it says behold,

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:15.660
the words of Joseph's brothers
before they killed Joseph.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:18.180
It says behold, here cometh the dreamer.

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:22.450
Let us slay him and see
what becomes of his dream.

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.470
And I really think that that's the moment

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:26.330
we're in right now in America,

00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:28.940
is what will become of our dream.

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:32.420
Heroes have been slain,
martyrs have been made.

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:35.180
But it's up to us, the living,
to answer that question,

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:36.810
because the dream of this country is,

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:38.210
we're still in search of it.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.880
We, it still beckons to
us from beyond our grasp.

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:45.600
And I'm just hoping that
we can be the nation

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:49.170
that together comes forward and says,

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:51.930
not on my watch will we
lose this dream, in fact,

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:54.100
we will advance it for
the next generation.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:57.870
And if we can do that now, I will,

00:29:57.870 --> 00:29:59.610
this could be the greatest era, often,

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:02.160
sometimes you have to
face the darkest of times

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:04.750
for America to lurch forward,
this could actually be one

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:06.978
of the greatest periods
right in front of us

00:30:06.978 --> 00:30:09.240
of national advancement where we make this

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:11.480
a more loving nation not just in words,

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.320
'cause sentimentality is easy.

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:16.200
But we can make it in
substance, in our laws.

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:18.730
And that's my hope, that's my dream.

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:22.550
Well, an absolute honor
to talk to you, Senator.

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:27.250
And I hear you and I think
a lot of people hear you.

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:30.070
And I think a lot of people are committed

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:35.070
to making this a day in, day out,

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:38.840
I'm gonna call it an exercise, struggle.

00:30:38.840 --> 00:30:42.760
But just through actions
rather than sentimentality

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:47.940
and then hoping someone
straightens this out,

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:51.050
because we all have to dive in.

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:54.220
Thank you, from your lips
to God's ears, thank you.

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:55.772
No one's listening to me.

00:30:55.772 --> 00:30:59.220
(Conan and Cory laugh)

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:01.465
That guy stopped listening
to me a long time ago

00:31:01.465 --> 00:31:03.313
or (laughter drowns out speaker) whatever.

00:31:04.435 --> 00:31:08.850
I don't know God's gender,
but God's seen too much

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:10.930
of my foolishness over the years.

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:13.420
But really, thank you so
much, I know you're very busy

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:15.810
and I know you're doing
very important work.

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:18.830
And so bless you for
taking the time today,

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:20.340
I really appreciate it.

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:22.210
Thank you, my friend, thank you so much.

00:31:22.210 --> 00:31:23.530
Take care, I salute you.

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:26.450
I don't even know if that's
proper, but I bow and I--

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:27.837
I will namaste you.

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:31.470
Next time we talk, I'll
have shaved my head.

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:35.240
Oh, that's exciting to me,
if that ever happened, truly,

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:37.430
you could pull one of those bald caps on,

00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:38.990
that would be enough.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:41.460
Maybe we'll do that, because
this thing, I'm sure,

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:43.520
is going to look hideous.

00:31:43.520 --> 00:31:45.680
You can pull it off, I cannot pull it off.

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:48.427
All right, have a great, have a great day,

00:31:48.427 --> 00:31:49.970
and thank you very much.

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:51.496
Thank you, thank you so much.

00:31:51.496 --> 00:31:52.620
Thank you, good night.

00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:53.453
Bye bye.

